      "notes": "All claims are well-supported by their cited evidence. Tier assignments are appropriate: measurable claims draw on factual baselines about the existing framework; projected claims are clearly forecasts. The 'improves/moderate/long-term' verdict is reasonable given the evidence \u2014 direct labour-standard improvements are real but primarily affect overseas supply chain workers rather than UK workers directly, and enforcement/scope remain undefined. Compliance cost counter-claims are included, supporting the moderate (not major) magnitude. No tier blurring, lever leakage, party identity, or false balance detected. One minor note: E4 is backed by advocacy sources (waronwant, impactpolicies, unseenuk) but the claim it supports (current framework covers only modern slavery) is factually grounded in law and corroborated by parliamentary sources elsewhere, so not blocking. E3's 40% figure is dated (2019) but the rationale appropriately uses it as a baseline illustration rather than a current precise figure."
